WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.509
Do you remember style transfer?

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:11.420
Style transfer is a mostly AI-based technique
where we take a photograph, put a painting

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.900
next to it, and it applies the style of the
painting to our photo.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.210
That was amazing.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.570
Also, do you remember pose estimation?

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:25.320
This is a problem where we have a photograph
or a video of someone, and the output is a

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.240
skeleton that shows the current posture of
this person.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.810
So how about something that combines the power
of pose estimation with the expressiveness

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:35.810
of style transfer?

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:41.810
For instance, this way, we could take a video
of a professional dancer, then record a video

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:48.310
of our own, let's say, moderately beautiful
moves, and then, transfer the dancer's performance

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:51.040
onto our own body in the video.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.010
Let's call it motion transfer.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.260
Have a look at these results.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.160
How cool is that?!

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:02.510
As you see, these output videos are quite
smooth, and this is not by accident.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.489
It doesn't just come out like that.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:08.280
With this technique, temporal coherence is
taken into consideration.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.930
This means that the algorithm knows what it
has done a moment ago and will not do something

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.800
wildly different, making these dance motions
smooth and believable.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.659
This method uses a generative adversarial
network, where we have a neural network for

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:28.490
pose estimation, or in other words, generating
the skeleton from an image, and a generator

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:33.830
network to create new footage when given a
test subject and a new skeleton posture.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:39.080
These two neural networks battle each other
and teach each other to distinguish and create

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.150
more and more authentic footage over time.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:47.150
Some artifacts are still there, but note that
this is among the first papers on this problem

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.040
and it is already doing incredibly well.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.500
This is fresh and experimental.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.730
Just the way I like it.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:58.299
Two followup papers down the line and we'll
be worried that we can barely tell the difference

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:00.040
from authentic footage.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.510
Make sure to have a look at the paper, where
you will see how the pix2pix algorithm was

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:09.209
also used for image generation and there is
a nice evaluation section as well.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:13.160
And now, let the age of AI-based dance videos
begin.

